<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Universal supercluster template · Laniakea · Shapley · Perseus‑Pisces · etc.</title>
    <style>
        * {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }
        body {
            background: #0b0f1a;
            background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 30%, #1b2c42 0%, #03050a 95%);
            font-family: 'Inter', 'Segoe UI', system-ui, sans-serif;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            padding: 2rem 1rem;
            color: #e2edff;
        }
        .container {
            max-width: 1300px;
            width: 100%;
            background: rgba(13, 22, 35, 0.9);
            backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
            -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
            border: 1px solid #3f618b;
            border-radius: 3.2rem;
            box-shadow: 0 30px 55px -15px #000000, 0 0 0 1px #6b8bb8 inset;
            padding: 2.8rem 3rem;
        }
        h1 {
            font-size: 4rem;
            font-weight: 400;
            letter-spacing: -0.02em;
            color: #ffedc0;
            text-shadow: 0 0 20px #b57c48;
            border-bottom: 3px solid #6c8ec0;
            padding-bottom: 0.8rem;
            margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
            display: flex;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            align-items: baseline;
            gap: 1.5rem;
        }
        h1 span {
            font-size: 2.2rem;
            font-weight: 300;
            color: #adc8ff;
            text-shadow: none;
        }
        h2 {
            font-size: 2.4rem;
            font-weight: 380;
            color: #fedb9f;
            border-left: 8px solid #c3975e;
            padding-left: 1.4rem;
            margin: 2.4rem 0 1.8rem 0;
        }
        h3 {
            font-size: 1.9rem;
            font-weight: 360;
            color: #eac084;
            margin: 2rem 0 1.2rem 0;
        }
        .content-section {
            background: #1a2b40dd;
            backdrop-filter: blur(5px);
            -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(5px);
            border: 1px solid #6082b0;
            border-radius: 2.8rem;
            padding: 2.2rem 2.5rem;
            margin-bottom: 2.8rem;
            box-shadow: 0 20px 30px -12px #000000b3;
        }
        .flex-row {
            display: flex;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            gap: 2.2rem 3.5rem;
            align-items: flex-start;
        }
        .quick-fact-item {
            background: #13324f;
            border-radius: 60px;
            padding: 0.7rem 2rem;
            margin: 0.6rem 0.6rem 0.6rem 0;
            display: inline-block;
            font-size: 1.15rem;
            border: 1px solid #85a7d1;
            color: #f4fbff;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 10px #00000040;
        }
        .quick-fact-item strong {
            color: #ffd78c;
            font-weight: 600;
            margin-right: 8px;
        }
        .coordinates-block {
            background: #0d2641;
            border-radius: 50px;
            padding: 1.2rem 2.2rem;
            margin: 1.5rem 0;
            font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', 'Fira Code', monospace;
            border: 1px solid #8daad3;
            display: inline-block;
            font-size: 1.2rem;
            color: #d9e8ff;
        }
        .image-placeholder {
            background: #1c3453;
            border-radius: 2.2rem;
            padding: 4rem 1.8rem;
            text-align: center;
            color: #b5d0ff;
            font-size: 1.2rem;
            border: 2px dashed #8ea9d4;
            box-shadow: inset 0 8px 20px #00000077;
            min-width: 200px;
            backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
        }
        .gallery {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
            gap: 2rem;
            margin: 2rem 0;
        }
        .gallery-item p {
            margin-top: 0.8rem;
            color: #c4d7ff;
            font-size: 1rem;
        }
        .data-table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            background: #1d3757;
            border-radius: 2.2rem;
            overflow: hidden;
            margin: 1.8rem 0;
            border: 1px solid #6384b7;
        }
        .data-table th {
            background: #244e78;
            color: #ffeec2;
            font-weight: 500;
            padding: 1.2rem 1.5rem;
            text-align: left;
            font-size: 1.1rem;
            width: 30%;
        }
        .data-table td {
            padding: 1.2rem 1.5rem;
            background: #1d3a5b;
            color: #e2f0ff;
            font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
            font-size: 1.05rem;
        }
        .badge-super {
            background: #b68b4b;
            color: #0b1425;
            padding: 0.4rem 1.6rem;
            border-radius: 60px;
            font-weight: 700;
            font-size: 0.9rem;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            display: inline-block;
            letter-spacing: 1px;
            border: 1px solid #ffdfaa;
        }
        .footer-note {
            margin-top: 3.5rem;
            text-align: center;
            color: #b9d0f0;
            background: #0e2843;
            padding: 1.4rem;
            border-radius: 70px;
            border: 1px solid #779ac9;
            font-size: 1.1rem;
        }
        hr {
            border: none;
            border-top: 3px dashed #6c89b5;
            margin: 2.5rem 0;
        }
        .spec-block {
            background: #1f3e61;
            border-radius: 2.5rem;
            padding: 2rem;
            margin: 1.8rem 0;
            border-left: 10px solid #e0a458;
        }
        .member-cluster-list {
            display: flex;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            gap: 0.8rem 1.5rem;
            margin-top: 1rem;
        }
        .member-cluster-list span {
            background: #153b5c;
            padding: 0.4rem 1.4rem;
            border-radius: 40px;
            border: 1px solid #8fb0dd;
            font-size: 1.1rem;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
    <!-- UNIVERSAL TITLE: supercluster name -->
    <h1>Laniakea Supercluster <span>(incl. Virgo, Hydra‑Centaurus, Pavo‑Indus)</span></h1>

    <!-- ========== SECTION 1: QUICK FACTS + COORDINATES (supercluster level) ========== -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center;">
            <div>
                <span class="badge-super">🌌 supercluster of galaxies</span>
            </div>
            <div class="quick-fact-item"><strong>richness class:</strong> 3 (very rich)</div>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <span class="quick-fact-item"><strong>Constellation (approx. centre):</strong> Centaurus / Hydra</span>
            <span class="quick-fact-item"><strong>Best observation season:</strong> southern autumn</span>
            <span class="quick-fact-item"><strong>Number of member clusters:</strong> ~300–500 galaxy clusters</span>
            <span class="quick-fact-item"><strong>Mass (total):</strong> ~10¹⁷ M☉</span>
        </div>

        <h3>🌐 Celestial reference (central region / centroid)</h3>
        <div class="coordinates-block">
            <strong>RA (approx):</strong> 12h 30m – 13h 00m &nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp; <strong>Dec:</strong> -40° to -45°<br>
            <strong style="margin-top:8px; display:inline-block;">Galactic coordinates (l, b):</strong> ~300° , +20°
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:1.8rem"><strong>🔭 large‑scale structure:</strong> part of the larger Pisces–Cetus Supercluster Complex? ; defined by Tully et al. (2014) using cosmic flows.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ========== SECTION 2: PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTICS (size, mass, redshift, dimensions) ========== -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2>📐 global properties</h2>

        <table class="data-table">
            <tr><th>Diameter (Mpc / Mly)</th><td>~160 Mpc (~520 Mly)</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Redshift range (z)</th><td>0.00 – 0.10 (core z ~ 0.016)</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Peculiar velocity / bulk flow</th><td>~630 km/s (Great Attractor region)</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Mass (dynamical / virial)</th><td>(5–10)×10¹⁶ M☉ (including dark matter)</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Luminosity (visible)</th><td>~5×10¹² L☉ (galaxy light only)</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Dominant components</th><td>Virgo Cluster, Hydra‑Centaurus, Pavo‑Indus, Fornax‑Eridanus</td></tr>
        </table>

        <div class="spec-block">
            <p><strong>✨ outstanding features:</strong> Laniakea defines the 'basin of attraction' – our Milky Way is part of it. The Great Attractor (Norma cluster) dominates the flow. Contains the Shapley Supercluster as a neighbouring massive structure.</p>
            <p><strong>🔭 key research:</strong> cosmic flows, dark matter distribution, effect on CMB (dipole), relation to the Dipole Repeller.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ========== SECTION 3: MEMBER CLUSTERS & MAJOR COMPONENTS ========== -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2>📋 major member clusters / components</h2>
        <div class="member-cluster-list">
            <span>Virgo Cluster</span> <span>Hydra Cluster</span> <span>Centaurus Cluster</span> <span>Pavo‑Indus Cluster</span>
            <span>Fornax Cluster</span> <span>Antlia Cluster</span> <span>Norma Cluster (Great Attractor)</span> <span>Abell 3521</span>
            <span>Abell 3565</span> <span>Abell 3574</span> <span>Abell 3581</span> <span>ESO 137-006</span> <span>... plus dozens of groups</span>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top:2rem;">📊 detailed table of main constituent clusters</h3>
        <table class="data-table">
            <tr><th>Cluster name</th><th>Distance (Mpc)</th><th>Richness</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Virgo Cluster</td><td>16.5</td><td>~1500 galaxies</td><td>nearest massive cluster; dominates local volume</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hydra Cluster (Abell 1060)</td><td>58</td><td>rich</td><td>triple merger?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Centaurus Cluster (Abell 3526)</td><td>45</td><td>rich</td><td>cool core, multiple subclumps</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Norma Cluster (Abell 3627)</td><td>68</td><td>very rich</td><td>core of Great Attractor, hidden by Milky Way</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pavo‑Indus Cluster</td><td>~70</td><td>intermediate</td><td>southern group</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem; font-style:italic;">(expand with more clusters as needed)</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ========== SECTION 4: HISTORY OF DISCOVERY / NAMING ========== -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2>📜 discovery & nomenclature</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 2.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;">
            <div style="flex:1;">
                <p><strong>Name origin:</strong> "Laniakea" (Hawaiian) meaning "immeasurable heaven" – proposed by R. Brent Tully et al. (2014) to define our supercluster based on velocity flows.</p>
                <p><strong>Earlier designations:</strong> previously considered part of the Local Supercluster or Virgo–Hydra–Centaurus Supercluster.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="flex:1;">
                <p><strong>Discovery / mapping:</strong> recognized gradually from redshift surveys (1980s–2010s). The Great Attractor was detected in the 1980s (Lynden‑Bell et al.). Laniakea defined using the COsmographic flow reconstruction.</p>
                <p><strong>Key publications:</strong> Tully et al. (2014) Nature, "The Laniakea supercluster of galaxies".</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ========== SECTION 5: GALLERY & VISUALISATIONS (large‑scale maps) ========== -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2>🖼️ visualisations & all‑sky maps</h2>
        <div class="gallery">
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <div class="image-placeholder">[ flow map / velocity field ]</div>
                <p>galaxy flow streamlines</p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <div class="image-placeholder">[ wedge diagram ]</div>
                <p>redshift wedge (decl. slice)</p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <div class="image-placeholder">[ all‑sky projection ]</div>
                <p>Laniakea boundary (Tully et al.)</p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <div class="image-placeholder">[ 3D model / video frame ]</div>
                <p>supercluster skeleton</p>
            </div>
            <div class="gallery-item">
                <div class="image-placeholder">[ Great Attractor ]</div>
                <p>Norma / hidden region</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem;">⬤ data sources: 2MRS, Cosmicflows-3, Planck, etc.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ========== SECTION 6: NEIGHBOURING SUPERCLUSTERS / LARGER SCALE ========== -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2>🌠 surrounding large structures</h2>
        <div class="flex-row">
            <div style="flex:1;">
                <p><strong>Shapley Supercluster:</strong> located behind Laniakea (z~0.05), mass ~2×10¹⁶ M☉; influences bulk flow.</p>
                <p><strong>Perseus‑Pisces Supercluster:</strong> opposite side of the sky, part of the 'Pisces‑Cetus' complex.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="flex:1;">
                <p><strong>Sculptor Void / CMB cold spot:</strong> adjacent low‑density region.</p>
                <p><strong>Dipole Repeller:</strong> proposed push from opposite direction (Hoffman et al.)</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ========== FOOTER ========== -->
    <p class="footer-note">
        ✦ supercluster template · Laniakea / Shapley / Perseus‑Pisces / Coma / Hercules · updated [Date]
    </p>

    <!-- ========== SUPPLEMENT: ADAPTATION FOR OTHER SUPERCLUSTERS ========== -->
    <hr>
    <div style="background: #1b385a; border-radius: 3.5rem; padding: 1.8rem 2.5rem;">
        <p style="font-weight:600; color:#fad893; font-size:1.3rem; margin-bottom:1rem;">📌  how to adapt for any supercluster</p>
        <ul style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.8rem 2.5rem; list-style: none; padding-left:0; color:#d8e7ff;">
            <li><strong>Shapley Supercluster:</strong> core at RA 13h30, Dec -30°; rich clusters A3558, A3562; many mergers.</li>
            <li><strong>Perseus‑Pisces:</strong> l~130°, b=-20°; contains Perseus Cluster (Abell 426), double chain.</li>
            <li><strong>Coma Supercluster:</strong> includes Coma (A1656) and Leo (A1367) clusters, z~0.02.</li>
            <li><strong>Hercules Supercluster:</strong> complex, several Abell clusters (A2147, A2151).</li>
            <li><strong>Sculptor Supercluster:</strong> near south galactic pole, loose association.</li>
            <li><strong>For any:</strong> adjust coordinates, membership table, and references to key surveys (SDSS, 6dF).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem; background:#23507a; border-radius:3rem; padding:0.8rem 1.8rem; color:#cfdefa;">→ also include mass estimates from X‑ray (if available) and velocity dispersion.</p>
    </div>
    <!-- end container -->
</div>
</body>
</html>